Benchmark measurement standard

This file is canonical for what benchmarks
mean: wire format, clocks, matching, the
endpoint model, patterns, sizes, needs,
and metric keys. docs/yaml stays the
schema reference: key shapes plus lint
bounds live there and it points back
here. Split in one line: meaning lives
here, shape lives in docs/yaml.

Wire format

Each weld issue sends one payload shaped

  bench#seq#send_ns#body

seq is the zero based per-step index.
send_ns is the sender CLOCK_MONOTONIC
reading in ns taken per issue at send
time. body is N x bytes when
payload_bytes is set, else the user text
verbatim. The tag alone stays below 64
bytes. Tag plus the largest body fits
the 66000 byte issue buffer, so builds
never truncate.

send_ns clock rule

Stamps come from CLOCK_MONOTONIC on the
sender, read fresh for every issue. A
clock failure reads zero and that issue
counts as loss, never as a sample. All
nodes on one host share the same
monotonic clock, so one-way latencies
compare directly. Across hosts the
clocks differ: bound the skew first, or
latency reads skewed by it.

Full-prefix matching rule

The observed path rebuilds the exact
full payload (tag plus body) and greps
the logs for that whole string. Matching
anchors on the full prefix, never on
content, so user text holding tag-like
text can never confuse it. Old two-field
tags (bench#N# plus text, no send_ns)
fail lint loudly and never parse.

Endpoint model

Dispatch runs on capability, not SUT.
The table names the verbs per kind:

  weld: pub via weld_pub; observe via
    weld log, plus the ROS capture where
    a peer runs beside the node; relay
    via weld_relay_start; never submits.
  ros: pub via ros_pub_once; observe via
    ROS log only; never relays.
  concord: no pub, log, or relay verb;
    submit via concord_submit, list via
    concord_list_has.

Pairing rule: from: names any kind with
a pub verb (weld or ros); on: names any
kind with an observe verb (weld dual
logs legacy, or ros only). Bare N means
the file default kind (weld in weld
files, concord in concord files);
weld:N is an alias for bare in weld
files; ros:N names the ROS peer;
concord:N names concord. The concord
submit family uses node:, image:, as:
instead and pairs only where verbs
exist: submit plus list.

Loud-failure rule: bad pairs fail lint
(plus a runtime backstop) naming the
missing verb, for example concord
cannot publish: no pub verb, concord
cannot observe: no log verb, ros cannot
relay: no relay verb. Adding a kind is
a table row, not new branches.

Traffic patterns

One-way: no echo: key. Publish from
from:, watch the full payload land on
on:. Where a ROS peer runs beside the
observed node, the ROS capture must hold
it too; with no peer, the weld log
alone decides. Concord matches the full
workload id in lists, so 8-char list
prefixes cannot collide; OUT_LEN
truncation may still miss, and the 10 s
pumped grace dwell covers it.

Roundtrip: echo: {by, node}. The run
starts a pong relay on the echo node
for the step, then stops it. The reply
topic is always <topic>/pong, where
topic is explicit or run derived. RTT is
reply sighting minus send, through the
same stats. by picks weld (dual logs)
or ros (ROS only), same rule as on;
concord by fails (no log verb). The
relay node must relay (weld only), and
echo needs relay.echo in needs.
Calibration note: the relay hop is part
of the measured RTT, so compare
roundtrip numbers against one-way
baselines on the same pair and build,
and keep relay placement fixed across
compared runs.

Stepped ladders: one benchmark per phase
at rising rate, count, or size; read
tables across phases to find the knee.
Multi-phase ladders still work and stay
the shape for concord sweeps.

Adaptive ramp: a ramp: block inside one
benchmark (never a mode: ramp key).
Fields: start_rate, step_rate,
step_duration_s, max_rate, max_steps
1..10, max_duration_s, plus loss_pct
0..100 and/or p99_ms 0..3600000; at
least one stop is required. Top level
rate, count, duration_s, and warmup are
forbidden with ramp. Per-step want is
rate times step_duration_s, capped like
non-ramp (weld 1000, concord 100);
concord total (steps times max rate
times step dur) stays below 128 by
lint. The executor runs the ladder:
measure a step, decide up or stop.
Bounds prove end: steps 1..10, time
1..3600 s, rate 1..1e6 rising by at
least 1, each step bounded by
step_duration plus grace.

Ceiling semantics: the top passing rate
is the ceiling. A passed step raises
it; the first stopped step ends the
ladder. The stop reason is one of loss,
p99, max_rate, max_steps, max_duration.

Payload sizes

payload_bytes is 0..65536; 0 or absent
means use text. A set body is N x bytes,
deterministic, never user text. Audit,
largest first: tag plus 65536 body plus
NUL fits the 66000 issue buffer; the
132000 file needle buffer holds the full
body, so end to end still matches; weld
pub argv fits (ARG_MAX 2 MB, per-string
128 KB, so 64 KB passes, 1 MB needs
chunking); ROS yaml holds tag plus
65536 (driver ROS_YAML_LEN), so both
sides sweep the same sizes; TUN
fragments at IP level
(MTU 1500, pump reads 2048), so large
still forwards; log pipes chunk at 2048
but file overlap still finds; the relay
pump uses getline with no cap. 64 KB
covers point clouds; 1 MB images need
chunking plus larger argv and DDS
checks. Sweeps are repeated phases with
different payload_bytes; no sweep
syntax exists.

Needs entries

Benchmarks use these file needs verbs:

  ros.pub: from: ros:N in a benchmark,
    or any ros_pub step.
  relay.echo: any echo: block.
  ros.peer: a beside peer where the
    observed path must also match the
    ROS capture.
  metrics.scrape: any prometheus-scrape
    expect. Generic, same for weld plus
    concord.
  workload.* plus journal.* plus spawn.*:
    as the queried conditions need;
    plain weld or concord benchmarks add
    no extra need of their own.

Metric keys

Every bench.* key rides metric events
only, through the shared metric site,
so TAP and JSON bytes never move. Units:
counts are issues, .ms leaves are whole
ms over seen ids (0 when none seen),
.us leaves are whole us over seen ids,
rate_ach is integer msgs per sec. p50
and p99 use nearest rank
ceil(pct*n/100) over ascending hits;
sub-ms ticks read 0 in ms, spread in
us. Loss never fails
the step; the phase condition still
gates pass.

Per-step leaves, bench.<ph>.<k>.*:

  count: non-warmup issued.
  loss: never seen in the 10 s pumped
    grace dwell, or total when the
    scrape gate fails.
  target: wanted non-warmup issues.
  overrun: 1 when issued fell short of
    wanted, else 0.
  p50.ms, p99.ms, max.ms: over seen ids.
  p50.us, p99.us, max.us: over seen ids
    in us.
  scrape_ok: 1 when the scrape threshold
    holds, 0 else; only with
    prometheus-scrape expect.
  rate_ach: total issued times 1e9 over
    issue loop ns (zero dwell reads
    total).
  lat: ms per seen id, at most 2000
    points per step, then stop.
  lat_us: us per seen id, same cap.
  truncated: 1 when lat hit the cap.
  hist.<b>: counts under fixed edges 1
    2 5 10 20 50 100 200 500 ms, 10
    buckets 0-1 up to 500 plus; only
    nonzero buckets emit.
  hist_us.<b>: counts under us edges
    100 500us, 1 5 10 20 50 100 500ms,
    10 buckets; only nonzero emit.

Per-ramp-step leaves,
bench.<ph>.<k>.ramp.<i>.*: count, loss,
target, overrun, p50.ms, p99.ms,
max.ms, p50.us, p99.us, max.us,
rate_ach. No lat or hist, to
keep logs bounded.

Summary leaves:
bench.<ph>.<k>.ceiling.rate is the top
passing rate, numeric.
ceiling.limited_by rides a marker event
(text would break numeric plots), one of
loss, p99, max_rate, max_steps,
max_duration.

